Yes—the mechanism you’re pointing at is real even without assuming a coordinated conspiracy.

When people have less direct contact with one another, more of their estimate of “what everyone thinks” comes through mediated signals: feeds, rankings, advertising, influencers, sales charts, recommendation systems, corporate messaging, news, and visible engagement. COVID-era isolation intensified that condition for many people, but the underlying phenomenon is older.

That creates a dangerous inversion:

\text{people want } X \rightarrow \text{companies make } X

can gradually become

\text{companies promote } X
\rightarrow
\text{people infer others want } X
\rightarrow
\text{people accommodate to perceived norm}
\rightarrow
X \text{ appears genuinely popular}

That is an information cascade mixed with social proof and pluralistic ignorance. Nobody individually needs to be lying for the aggregate signal to become misleading.

GPT Polls could attack exactly that problem by separating three things that are usually collapsed:

Private preference — “What do you actually like?”

Perceived social preference — “What do you think everyone else likes?”

Observed behavior — “What are people actually choosing?”

Then calculate the deltas:

\Delta_1 =
P(\text{I like X})
-
P(\text{I think others like X})

\Delta_2 =
P(\text{stated preference})
-
P(\text{observed choice})

Those gaps are incredibly informative.

Imagine:

Do you like ultra-minimalist gray interiors?
Personally like: 29%
Think most people like: 68%
Believe designers prefer: 81%

Suddenly you’ve discovered something much more valuable than an approval rating:

People may be designing for an audience that largely exists inside everyone’s model of everyone else.

That becomes a core GPT Polls feature:

Mirror Poll

Ask every respondent two questions:

What do you want?

What do you think other people want?

Then expose the discrepancy.

You could even add:

What do you think companies believe people want?

Now you’ve got three recursive models:

Person
\rightarrow
Model(Persons)
\rightarrow
Model(Company's\ Model(Persons))

Compare those against actual behavior.

That’s almost a semantic price-discovery mechanism for preferences.

Instead of corporations or cultural gatekeepers having privileged access to market research and everybody else having to infer consensus from whatever reaches their screen, ordinary people get access to a representation of the preference field too.

But the Anchor Spine we just discussed becomes essential here. GPT Polls couldn’t allow advertisers, sponsors, or its own previous predictions to quietly contaminate the modeled population, or it would recreate exactly the phenomenon it was intended to measure.

A strong invariant would therefore be:

Measure preference separately from attempts to shape preference.

And another:

Never present perceived consensus as observed consensus.

Then the information flow becomes bidirectional again:

people \leftrightarrow producers

rather than merely:

producer \rightarrow perception \rightarrow consumer

The interesting part of your idea isn’t really “AI can tell me what’s popular.”

It’s:

give individuals some of the population-level vision that institutions already use to make decisions about individuals.

That is where the omnidirectionality matters.